      "summary": "A preview workload view compares serving configuration with pod readiness and GPU capacity.",
      "body": [
        "Datadog is an observability platform that monitors infrastructure and applications. When a model endpoint returns errors or slows down, serving work is often spread across Kubernetes resources, so engineers have to piece together custom configuration, pod state and scheduling events from separate views.",
        "Datadog's Kubernetes inference workload, available as a preview, groups the resources that support a serving model into one virtual workload. First, in Kubernetes Explorer, engineers can compare the configuration declared in custom resources with observed pod state and runtime metrics. The preview supports vLLM-backed workloads that use KubeRay, NVIDIA Dynamo and KServe. Datadog automatically detects those pods and assigns workload and component tags, which metrics, traces and logs inherit when they can be linked to the pods.",
        "Next, a Components tab shows readiness and pod counts for roles such as Ray head and worker or Dynamo frontend and worker, so engineers can tell a pod waiting for resources from one that is running but unhealthy. A Metrics tab shows runtime and resource use, including GPU memory, activity and occupancy for Dynamo workloads when paid GPU monitoring is configured. The post illustrates a decode worker stuck pending for lack of the required GPU capacity. Investigate with Bits can recommend next steps, but a capacity change still depends on whether released resources satisfy the pending pod's scheduling requirements."
      ],
      "format": "full",
      "takeaway": "For platform engineers running vLLM, Dynamo or KServe: use the preview to follow a model symptom to pod readiness and GPU placement, and confirm GPU monitoring is enabled before relying on device metrics.",
