      "summary": "A Codex integration reports usage, attributed code and review activity, and can compare delivery outcomes.",
      "body": [
        "Datadog is an observability platform that collects metrics, traces and logs. Engineering leaders who give teams access to OpenAI Codex, a coding agent, still may not see how people use it, how much of its code they accept, or whether its review comments are useful.",
        "The OpenAI Codex integration collects per-user activity from the Codex Analytics API and reports it as Datadog metrics. First, an overview dashboard tracks threads (conversations a developer starts) and turns (messages in those conversations), plus consumed Codex credits and tokens. Credits reflect credit consumption, not spend. Usage can be filtered by workspace, user, model or client, and token use can be split into cached input, uncached input and output, but not by client.",
        "Next, the dashboard plots lines of code added and removed that are attributable to Codex, and it tracks reviews and responses such as upvotes and downvotes. Those signals can be sent to Datadog AI Impact, which classifies pull requests as AI-assisted or not and compares throughput, cycle time, change failure rate and recovery time. Commits count as AI-assisted when Codex reports that the engineer created lines of code that day; a pull request counts if it contains at least one such commit. Setup requires a ChatGPT Enterprise workspace with Codex enabled and an admin key that can read the analytics API."
      ],
      "format": "full",
      "takeaway": "For engineering leaders rolling out Codex: confirm the workspace and key requirements, then compare usage with attributed code and delivery outcomes rather than treating access as adoption.",
